section('tpot','TPOT stall evidence and regression question','''<p>The repeat has ten requests above 40 ms TPOT and four above 80 ms. Its whole-run p99 is 17.236 ms, but the worst 20-second p99 is 83.391 ms. A small cluster of stalled streams can therefore dominate a short-window p99 without changing the whole-run p99 much.</p>
<p>At approximately 350.937–352.463 elapsed seconds, four active streams share a 1.526-second gap in all observed token receipts. Sixteen new requests are dispatched during that gap. This argues against a complete freeze of the load-generator event loop. The exact trace joins below further localize the delay.</p>
<p><strong>The stall pattern existed before the retained source patches:</strong> the depth-2 baseline already had three individual TPOT values above 80 ms; the depth-1 configuration-only trial had a 90.31 ms short-window p99. This disproves that the final map-copy patch first introduced the phenomenon. It does not establish that the patches have zero effect on its frequency or clustering.</p>
<p>Whole-run TPOT p99 improved from 21.39 ms in the original depth-2 baseline to 17.34 / 17.24 ms in the final runs. Exact joins recovered 37 traces / 1,998 spans. The ten TPOT-tail requests have Decode-forward durations of 0.189–0.334 seconds. Every enclosing Decode request ends 1.022–1.460 seconds after its Decode Scheduler span ends, measured within the same SGLang service. This avoids relying on client/server clock alignment for that gap. The evidence localizes much of the stall after Decode scheduling, in output handling/delivery; it does not yet identify the blocking function.</p>
<p>The TTFT-hot cohort instead has mean Prefill waiting of 2.091 seconds, versus 0.098 seconds in a nearby control cohort. At the worst TTFT chart point, TPOT p99 is only 16.56 ms. At the worst TPOT point, TTFT p50 is 2.995 seconds. The two spike mechanisms should be investigated separately. Both final runs have identical 20-second arrival-window uncached-load ranges of approximately 2.794–3.172M TPM, which weakens a large offered-load burst as the explanation.</p>
<p><a href="trace-cohort-summary.json">Trace cohort summary</a> · <a href="decompose_spike_traces.py">Reproduction script</a>. Exact request/span identifiers remain in the private trace evidence directory. Python garbage collection remains one candidate mechanism, not an established cause.</p>''')
section('followup','Follow-up diagnostic and next controlled trial','''<p>ATT21, '''+reportlink('20260913_023856')+''', completed after an unchanged-runtime 100-request warmup. All 5,921 requests succeeded with complete TTFT, output and DONE; identities and input/cache counts matched ATT20. The generator was valid, all 22 observed error/rejection series had zero increments, 56 queue gauges were idle twice, and all three original containers remained Ready with zero restarts. Its collectors are closed.</p>'''+table(['ATT21 diagnostic metric','Result'],[['Global TTFT p50 / p99','2.533 / 4.554 seconds'],['Worst 20s completion TTFT p50','4.299 seconds at 444s; 163 requests; 28 chart points above 4s'],['Global / worst 20s TPOT p99','17.394 / 52.658 ms'],['Individual TPOT tails','7 requests above 40ms; 3 above 80ms'],['CPU capture','Two nonblocking 10Hz recordings lasting 660s; Decode Python PID 1 and detokenizer PID 366'],['Capture quality','PID 1: 33,064 thread samples / 130 read errors; PID 366: 19,683 thread samples / 38 errors']])+'''<p>This is diagnostic evidence, not an uninstrumented performance acceptance run. Most main-thread samples ended at the native event-loop boundary; detokenizer samples mostly waited for input. The capture did not isolate a long Python loop. Native stacks were absent, and failed reads plus synthetic sample weights prevent exact wall-time attribution. No explicit GC sample does not rule out garbage collection.</p>
<p>The sampled serving path is the older Dynamo <code>request_handlers/llm/decode_handler.py</code>, not the colocated newer <code>llm_engine.py</code>. Active handler/main source files and hashes were preserved. Any startup-freeze proposal must be based on the active initialization path. <a href="analyze_output_profiles.py">CPU-sample analysis script</a>; private profiles are in <code>attempt21-output-delivery-diagnostic/cpu-stacks/</code>.</p>
<p><strong>ATT22 is a failed, interrupted trial:</strong> '''+reportlink('20260913_030350')+'''. Prefill scheduling size changed from 8,192 to 6,144 tokens with a matching graph bucket. Exact source, images, memory/cache settings, depth 1, output cap 20 and workload remained fixed. The initial recent p50 was about 1.8 seconds, but sustained Prefill backlog developed. Admissions stopped at 341.732 seconds. Of 3,289 admitted requests, 3,276 succeeded and 13 received HTTP 529 overload responses; 2,632 planned requests were intentionally not admitted.</p>'''+table(['ATT22 interrupted trial','Observed result'],[['Successful-request TTFT p50 / p99','4.627 / 17.139 seconds'],['Worst 20s completion TTFT p50','16.818 seconds at 357s; 202 samples; 218 chart points above 4s'],['Global / worst 20s TPOT p99','17.380 / 56.393 ms'],['Load generator','Valid; all admitted client outcomes accounted for'],['Residual serving state','13 pending Prefill bootstraps per PP rank after client completion; Decode and frontend client gauges idle'],['Recovery','Exact saved 8K recipe restored and Ready; retrieval correctness anomaly under investigation']])+'''<p>The 6K configuration did not sustain enough Prefill throughput at the fixed offered load. The frontend rejection/error counters increased by 13; sampled backend rejection counters did not increase. Residual Prefill bootstraps are consistent with the previously reported cleanup bug after overload. No cleanup fix is claimed.</p>
<p>The old drain watcher and all collectors were closed before recovery. The record explicitly distinguishes complete client drain from incomplete engine drain; the replacement worker cannot retroactively make the failed trial appear drained. The recovery diff restores only the three Prefill argument values. An initial preflight stopped before mutation because the default Kubernetes API schema differed from the chart schema; the corrected check reads the explicit API conversion.</p>
<p><a href="diagnostic-att21.json">ATT21 aggregate evidence</a> · <a href="negative-att22.json">ATT22 partial-run evidence</a> · <a href="prefill6k-values.json">Exact 6K values override</a>. The interrupted trial is excluded from the full-run comparison table. Its truncated percentiles are not directly comparable to complete ten-minute trials. Since the TPOT stall pattern predates the retained source patches, its unresolved root cause can take lower priority while testing the remaining TTFT spikes.</p>''')
section('recovery-correctness','Recovery retrieval anomaly: unresolved','''<div class="callout"><strong>Performance tuning is paused for a correctness investigation.</strong> After restoring the exact 8K configuration, the third synthetic retrieval probe generated repeated digits, reached its 256-token limit and produced no final answer. The first two probes returned the expected identifiers. This is preserved as a failed correctness check, not discarded as a short-budget test.</div>
<p>The worker is Ready with unchanged source/image hashes, fixed KV capacity and all four 8K graph captures. A fresh-cache repeat returned all three correct identifiers; its exact-text comparison differed only in whitespace. A later pass does not explain or erase the first failure. A predeclared five-trio series then passed all 15 requests after owned-cache resets, with exact input/cache coverage. These later passes do not establish the original cause. A source control restored both Prefill files to their exact frozen pre-optimization versions, keeping the 8K settings, other overlays, images and Decode/frontend containers fixed. Its predeclared series returned 14 correct answers out of 15. The first third-cold probe stopped after only one identifier, although all protocol completions and input/cache counts were correct; the following 12 requests passed. All four native 8K graphs executed. The retained patches are therefore not necessary for this fixture to exhibit a retrieval failure. The two observed failure modes differ, and these small series do not establish the exact cause or prove zero patch effect.</p>
<p>Private evidence: <code>/mnt/HPC/tom/pd-latency-followup-20260913/recovery-after-att22/retrieval-candidate.json</code>, <code>retrieval-repeat01-candidate.json</code>, and <code>retrieval-series-retained8k/</code>. The frozen control and all logs are under <code>attempt23-frozen-source-correctness/</code>. Existing successful latency runs remain valid observations of speed, but do not constitute comprehensive answer-correctness validation. Further diagnosis will distinguish KV transfer consistency from generation behavior; no full load run has been started on the frozen control.</p>''')
section('kv-boundary','A concrete KV handoff mismatch','''<div class="callout"><strong>ATT25 reproduced the third-request failure with a byte-level difference.</strong> Two retrieval answers were correct; the third cold request reached 256 tokens without a final answer. Its regular KV hashes differ between Prefill and Decode in global layers 58–77, exactly the last Prefill pipeline stage. No performance replay followed this finding.</div>
<p>The first two requests match all 156 logical pool tensors each. The failed request matches 136 of 156: all 78 DSA key/scale tensors match, while 20 regular KV tensors differ. The final 16 token rows match even in those 20 tensors; earlier Decode bytes contain substantially more zeros. Fifteen boundary snapshots join by exact internal request and bootstrap-room identity, covering about 3.0 GB of logical KV bytes. These observations suggest an early chunk read before its GPU writes complete, but do not yet establish the precise missing range or prove the cause.</p>
<p>Source inspection found a relevant existing dependency gap: with pipeline parallelism, this deployment disables the ordinary overlap scheduler. Its non-final chunk send can precede the prior forward-completion wait; the last pipeline rank lacks the proxy-send dependency present on the other ranks. ATT26 then added a broad producer-completion wait only to Prefill. All three answers were correct and all 468 compared pool tensors matched, while Decode and frontend remained unchanged. The expected zero hidden-state layout difference persisted. This supports the missing dependency as the cause of the observed KV mismatch; three requests with synchronous fingerprint diagnostics do not establish general correctness. ATT27 replaces the broad scheduler wait with a per-segment CUDA event consumed in Mooncake’s transfer thread, after outstanding-transfer accounting and before an abort/status recheck. Thirteen CPU fixtures execute the actual send, queue and worker methods. ATT27 also returned all three correct answers with all 468 pool tensor comparisons matching. Six prior Decode metadata files were hash-verified and excluded from this new trial. All queues drained. This change is not yet a validated performance result; the isolated Prefill151/Decode192 deployment subsequently passed all 15 predeclared retrieval requests with fingerprint capture disabled. This sequential correctness result is separate from sustained-load performance.</p>
<p>All observed metadata differences concern zero-filled hidden-state buffers with different layouts: 16 float32 values on Prefill versus 6,144 bfloat16 values on Decode. This difference appears on both passing and failing requests. The six other compared fields match. Zero speculative-seed validity remains a separate question; the layout difference alone is not evidence of random transport corruption.</p>
<p>ATT24 sent no probes. Before using its diagnostic, review found that the original DSA fingerprint helper included padding and omitted valid scale bytes on partial pages. A diagnostic-only correction passed twelve CPU fixtures, including a deliberately changed final scale byte that the original helper missed. An opt-in guard limits captures to the P/D boundary, preserving the existing EAGLE configuration without per-verify dumps. These are diagnostic changes, separate from the six retained performance changes.</p>
<p><a href="kv-boundary-att25.json">Aggregate comparison and limitations</a> · <a href="diagnostic-fingerprint.patch">Diagnostic helper patch</a>. Exact source hashes, CPU fixtures, rollout manifests, all request outcomes and snapshot archives are retained under <code>/mnt/HPC/tom/pd-latency-followup-20260913/attempt25-pd-only-kv-fingerprint/</code> and <code>candidates/dsa-snapshot-layout/</code>. Hashing synchronizes CUDA and may alter a race; these request timings are not performance evidence.</p>''')
section('expanded-goal','Expanded four-node investigation','''<p>Tom added nodes 151 and 192 to 130 and 132. The goal now includes a 2P1D deployment at about 60M input / 6M uncached TPM with the same roughly 90% hit rate, ten-minute measured duration, 20-token output cap and latency/speed requirements. The target remains every Alex 20-second completion-window TTFT p50 below 4 seconds, correct outputs and zero errors, with TPOT tails investigated separately.</p>
<p>Initial checks found all four GPUs idle on each new node; all sixteen GPUs share a healthy NVLink fabric. An isolated second 1P1D deployment was installed at 12:37 Singapore for Prefill151 / Decode192, with a private 600GiB Mooncake Store and frontend port 32109. All workers became Ready and the uninstrumented correctness control passed 15/15 requests, exact cache/input coverage, and all-rank 8K graph replay. ATT28 now rolls only Prefill151 to the six optimized source changes plus producer events on both send paths; Decode192, frontend and Store are unchanged. Sustained latency testing remains pending. The tentative final topology is Prefill132 + Prefill151 and one eight-GPU Decode spanning130 +192.</p>
<p>The fourth doubled-load selection is registered in Alex as <code>tom-capacity-v3-2p-60m-6m-10min-20260913</code>. It has 13,469 measured requests from1,254 sessions:600,193,610 input and59,992,906 modeled uncached tokens over600 seconds. Its400 distinct warmup requests add22,317,642 input tokens over120 seconds. Every one of72,325 numeric boundary probes passes the disclosed1% input/5% uncached whole-request tolerances. Sampled rolling minutes have at least335 exact16K prefix families; largest-family share is at most3.225%.</p>
<p>Alex Prepare <code>20260913_045325</code> finished at12:55:20 Singapore using16CPUworkers in112seconds, with no inference requests. All13,869 original payload frames were hash/decompression/JSON checked; all canonical bodies are distinct. Every prepared token sequence equals its verified original GLM5.3 source. Dataset ID:<code>dataset-dfa320022c5b1e8c171a2227</code>; token profile:<code>profile-0f6d63bca1c499991a2a2073</code>. The full-pack picker average51.878MTPM includes warmup; the measured schedule targets60M.</p>
<p>Runtime cache behavior is still unverified. The modeled two-Prefill configuration has11.102528M local tokens each and20M shared logical L3 tokens. Ten seconds of completion delay plus five seconds of L3 publication delay passes the round-robin audit; twenty seconds of total visibility delay fails. Actual Store byte capacity, routing, pinning and L3 hit partitioning require live checks. Earlier candidates remain recorded: candidate01 fails the five-second publication sensitivity; candidates02 and03 fail seven and two sampled numeric windows respectively. Candidate04 bounds cumulative uncached-token debt across minute boundaries; it does not retime failed windows after selection.</p>
<p><a href="optimized-event.patch">Producer-event patch on top of the six optimizations</a>. The deferred path records the event before launching later independent forward work. Thirteen actual-method transport fixtures,51 ownership/lifetime checks and six producer-boundary checks passed on CPU. The first uninstrumented control and this combined candidate have separate records; neither is a new best latency run.</p>
<p><a href="doubled-load-candidate-summary.json">Dataset counts, numeric audits and cache scenarios</a> · <a href="producer-fence-att26.json">ATT26 comparison</a> · <a href="producer-event-att27.json">ATT27 comparison</a> · <a href="async-kv-event.patch">ATT27 event-fence patch</a> · <a href="async-kv-event-cpu.json">Thirteen CPU fixtures</a>. Private manifests, source hashes, startup evidence and failed candidates are retained under <code>/mnt/HPC/tom/pd-latency-followup-20260913/expanded-four-node/</code> and <code>attempt27-async-kv-event/</code>.</p>''')
